+## F-3 — HIGH: every withdrawal permanently strands the withdrawal fee. The gap grows with use.
+
+In `withdraw()`:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 grossAssets = convertToAssets(shares);
+uint256 fee = (grossAssets * WITHDRAW_FEE_BPS) / BPS_DENOMINATOR;
+netAssets = grossAssets - fee;
+...
+totalAssetsStored -= grossAssets;     // the full amount leaves the books
+aavePool.withdraw(address(usdt), netAssets, msg.sender);   // only the net leaves Aave
+```
+
+`grossAssets` is deducted from the accounting but only `netAssets` is pulled out of Aave. The
+`fee` stays in Aave and is simultaneously erased from `totalAssetsStored` — so it belongs to
+nobody. The NatSpec at the top of the file claims both fees "stay supplied in Aave and accrue
+to the remaining shareholders rather than being swept to the owner." They stay supplied. They
+do not accrue to anyone.
+
+I found this with a handler-driven invariant asserting that accounting equals custody. Note
+`fail_on_revert = true` and `reverts: 0` in the table — the sequences reached real states.
+
+To reproduce the exact counterexample below, run the per-file command with `--fuzz-seed 1` as
+shown and clear any stale `cache/invariant/` first (`rm -rf cache/invariant`), or Foundry
+replays the previously persisted failure instead of searching. Running the whole suite at once
+shifts the fuzz stream and yields a different — equally failing — counterexample.
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/invariant/VaultInvariant.t.sol' --fuzz-seed 1
+Ran 2 tests for test/invariant/VaultInvariant.t.sol:VaultInvariantTest
+[FAIL: vault accounting diverged from aUSDT actually held: 118823331567 != 118824419462]
