+## F-4 — HIGH: `setDepositFee` has no upper bound. Values ≥ 10,000 brick the vault.
+
+`setDepositFee` writes `newFeeBps` straight to storage with no validation. The accepted domain
+is `[0, 2**256-1]`. In `deposit()`, `netAmount = amount - (amount * depositFeeBps) / 10_000`.
+
+I fuzzed the parameter across its domain with `bound()`. The property asserted is simply that
+any fee the owner is *allowed* to set leaves the vault usable:
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/fuzz/DepositFeeFuzz.t.sol' --fuzz-seed 1
+Ran 6 tests for test/fuzz/DepositFeeFuzz.t.sol:DepositFeeFuzzTest
+[FAIL: panic: arithmetic underflow or overflow (0x11); counterexample: calldata=0x2c2ca8c500000000000000000000000000000000000000000e97bfe582c6e66135e12d6f args=[4516244141986733524760210799 [4.516e27]]] testFuzz_AnyAcceptedFeeLeavesDepositUsable(uint256) (runs: 0, μ: 0, ~: 0)
+[FAIL: depositor lost more than 5% of principal to the deposit fee: 369343660 < 950000000; counterexample: calldata=0x396f67700000000000000010f80f4d6ca7883c8e4b80ccf8c9f16ba79fb3f8e13332df37 args=[106516035754831192515871295767113074787700948300713054887735 [1.065e59]]] testFuzz_DepositorRetainsValue(uint256) (runs: 0, μ: 0, ~: 0)
+[PASS] test_Boundary_Fee10000_BricksDeposits() (gas: 30565)
+[PASS] test_Boundary_Fee10001_BricksDepositsByUnderflow() (gas: 28538)
+[FAIL: 99.99% deposit fee accepted: 199980 < 950000000] test_Boundary_Fee9999_ConfiscatesDeposit() (gas: 344212)
+[PASS] test_SetDepositFeeAcceptsMaxUint() (gas: 19935)
+
+Suite result: FAILED. 3 passed; 3 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 1.86ms (2.68ms CPU time)
+
+Ran 1 test suite in 7.78ms (1.86ms CPU time): 3 tests passed, 3 failed, 0 skipped (6 total tests)
+```
+
+The three boundary cases around `BPS_DENOMINATOR` are classified separately because they fail
+through **different paths** — the three `[PASS]` lines are tests asserting the bad behaviour, so
+they document it rather than contradict it:
+
+| Fee | Behaviour |
+| --- | --- |
+| **9,999** — nearest value below the limit | Accepted, and deposits still "work". Alice deposits 1,000 USDT and ends up with **0.19998 USDT** redeemable. The other 999.8 is handed to the existing shareholders. |
+| **10,000** — the exact limit | Accepted by `setDepositFee`. `netAmount` is 0, `shares` is 0, `deposit` reverts with `NoSharesMinted()`. Vault bricked. |
+| **10,001** — first value beyond | Accepted by `setDepositFee`. `amount - fee` underflows, `deposit` reverts with **panic 0x11**. Bricked through a different path — note that a `try/catch` or an off-chain error handler keyed on `NoSharesMinted` would not see this one. |
+| **2**<sup>**256**</sup>**-1** | `setDepositFee(type(uint256).max)` succeeds without reverting. |
+
+`10,000` is not a semantically usable value — a 100% fee is not a fee, it is confiscation — so
+the real limit needs to sit well below it.
+
+**Fix.** Add `uint256 public constant MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS = 100;` (1%, or whatever the product
+actually intends) and `if (newFeeBps > MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS) revert FeeTooHigh();` at the top of
+`setDepositFee`. Validate `_depositFeeBps` in the constructor against the same constant — right
+now a deploy script typo ships a broken vault with no on-chain complaint. Keep the fuzz test:
+a hardcoded `setDepositFee(10_001)` test would have been written only because someone already
+suspected the bug; the fuzzer found it without being told.
+
