+## F-5 — HIGH: the owner can seize a pending deposit by front-running it.
+
+F-4 is worse than a footgun because the fee applies to the deposit *in the same block it is
+changed*, and the confiscated principal goes to existing shareholders — which the owner can be.
+A compromised owner key (or a malicious operator) watches the mempool, raises the fee to 9,999,
+and lets the victim's deposit land behind it.
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/OwnerPowers.t.sol' -vv
+Ran 2 tests for test/OwnerPowers.t.sol:OwnerPowersTest
+[FAIL: owner extracted value from another user's deposit: 100000000000 != 1000000000] test_OwnerCannotFrontRunDepositToSeizePrincipal() (gas: 393521)
+Logs:
+  alice deposited               100000000000
+  alice redeemable              1000000000
+  owner redeemable before       1000000000
+  owner redeemable after        100000000000
+  owner gain                    99000000000
+
+[PASS] test_PauseDoesNotTrapExistingDeposits() (gas: 201344)
+Logs:
+  alice's shares locked while paused: 9990000000000000000000
+
+Suite result: FAILED. 1 passed; 1 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 1.01ms (429.55µs CPU time)
+
+Ran 1 test suite in 7.64ms (1.01ms CPU time): 1 tests passed, 1 failed, 0 skipped (2 total tests)
+```
+
+The owner seeds a 1,000 USDT position, raises the fee, and Alice's 100,000 USDT deposit leaves
+her with 1,000 redeemable while the owner's claim goes from 1,000 to 100,000. **The owner
+gained 99,000 USDT of Alice's money in one transaction.** There is no timelock, no two-step
+ownership transfer, and `Ownable` is a single EOA.
+
+**Fix.** Three layers, all standard: (1) the `MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS` cap from F-4 bounds the
+worst case; (2) put `setDepositFee` behind a timelock, or at minimum use `Ownable2Step` with a
+multisig owner so a single key compromise is not sufficient; (3) give `deposit` a
+`minSharesOut` slippage parameter so a depositor's transaction reverts rather than executing at
+terms that changed after they signed. (3) is the one that actually closes the front-run.
+
+---
+
+## F-6 — MEDIUM: `pause()` freezes withdrawals, trapping deposits indefinitely.
+
+`withdraw()` carries `whenNotPaused`. The `[PASS]` on `test_PauseDoesNotTrapExistingDeposits`
+above is that test confirming the lock: while paused, Alice cannot burn her 9,990e18 shares —
+the call reverts with `Pausable.EnforcedPause`. The owner can pause and never unpause, and
+there is no timelock or expiry on the pause.
+
+This may be intentional, but it means "the owner can freeze all user funds forever" is a live
+property of the deployment, and it is the standard finding auditors flag as a rug vector.
+
+**Fix.** Drop `whenNotPaused` from `withdraw()`. A pause should stop new risk from entering, not
+stop users from leaving. If you must be able to halt withdrawals during an Aave incident, make
+it a separate, explicitly-named guardian action with a hard expiry.
+
+---
+
+## Two further items, noted without a failing test
+
+Flagging these for completeness; I did not build a reproduction for either, so treat them as
+review observations rather than demonstrated defects.
+
+- **`aavePool.withdraw`'s return value is discarded.** Aave returns the amount actually
+  withdrawn, which can be less than requested if the reserve is illiquid or the reserve is
+  frozen. The vault burns the user's shares regardless and emits an event claiming the full
+  `netAssets` was sent. Check the return value and revert on a short withdrawal.
+- **Shares are a bare mapping.** `shareBalance` has no transfer, no approval and no ERC-20
+  surface, so positions are non-transferable and invisible to every wallet and explorer. The
+  `ERC4626` migration suggested under F-2 resolves this too.
+
+---
+
+## Bottom line
+
+| ID | Severity | Summary | Reachable on mainnet? |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| F-1 | Critical | `deposit()` reverts on real USDT — vault is inoperable | Every deposit, always |
+| F-2 | Critical | Aave yield never accrues to depositors; a 1-year deposit loses money | Every deposit |
+| F-3 | High | Withdrawal fee permanently stranded; gap grows with use | Every withdrawal |
+| F-4 | High | Unbounded `setDepositFee`; ≥ 10,000 bricks the vault | Owner call |
+| F-5 | High | Owner can front-run a deposit and take the principal | Owner call |
+| F-6 | Medium | `pause()` traps user funds with no expiry | Owner call |
+
+F-1 alone means Monday's deployment cannot take a deposit at all — which, ironically, is the
+only reason F-2 through F-5 would not immediately cost users money. Fixing F-1 in isolation
+would make the vault operational and the rest of these live.
+
+The suite is green because it tests a mock of USDT rather than USDT, single operations rather
+than sequences, and two hand-picked fee values rather than the domain the setter accepts. The
+100% coverage number is accurate and tells you nothing about any of the six findings above.
